1 NOTE {geni:about_me} https://it.wikipedia.org/wiki/Niccol%C3%B2_I_Ludovisi
2 CONT
2 CONT ====Golden Fleece - Knights: 1430-1714 (in 1657)====
2 CONT * [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Niccol%C3%B2_Ludovisi Wikipedia - '''Niccolò Ludovisi''']
2 CONT * Not to be confused with Cardinal Niccolò Albergati-Ludovisi
2 CONT
2 CONT Niccolò I Ludovisi (1610– 25 December 1664) was Prince of Piombino from 1634 until his death.
2 CONT ====Family====
2 CONT He was the son of Orazio Ludovisi, patrician of Bologna and commander-in-chief of the Papal Army (as well as brother of Pope Gregory XV), and Lavinia Albergati. He was the nephew of later-Cardinal Niccolò Albergati-Ludovisi. He wa
2 CONC s a brother of Ludovico Ludovisi who was made a cardinal by their uncle the pope.
2 CONT ====Marriages====
2 CONT Ludovisi was married three times. 
2 CONT # After a first marriage (30 November 1622) with Isabella Gesualdo, princess of Venosa, 
2 CONT # in 1632 Niccolò married Polissena Appiani, the daughter and heiress of Isabella Appiani, Princess of Piombino.
2 CONT # His third marriage in 1644 was to Costanza Pamphili, niece of Pope Innocent X  and the sister of Camillo Pamphili who married Ludovisi's niece, Olimpia Aldobrandini.
2 CONT ====Children====
2 CONT Niccolo Ludovisi had seven children:
2 CONT * with his first wife Isabella Gesualdo (1611–1629):
2 CONT # Lavinia (1627–1634), died in childhood
2 CONT * with his second wife Polissena Appiani (?-1642):
2 CONT # Gregorio Filippo (1633-c.1637), died in childhood
2 CONT * with his third wife Costanza Pamphili (1627–1665):
2 CONT # Giovan Battista (1647–1699), Prince of Piombino from 1664
2 CONT # Olimpia (1656–1700), Princess of Piombino (1700), unmarried
2 CONT # Lavinia (1659–1682), married Giangirolamo, Duke of Atri but had no issue
2 CONT # Ippolita (1663–1733), Princess of Piombino (1700–1733), married Gregorio II Boncompagni and had issue
2 CONT # Niccolo (c.1664-1665), died in infancy
2 CONT ====Titlels====
2 CONT Niccolò was recognized prince in 1634 after paying one million florins. He also inherited his father's titles, becoming marquis of Populonia and duke of Fiano.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was Spanish viceroy in Aragon (1660–1662) and Sardinia (1662–1664) and in '''1657''' he was named a '''Knight of the Order of the Golden Fleece'''.
2 CONT
2 CONT Niccolò Ludovisi died at Cagliari, and was succeeded in his state by his son Giovan Battista Ludovisi.
